*Definition* $quad$ Let $(G_i)_(i in I)$ be a family of groups. Endow $display(product_(i in I) G_i)$ with the binary operation $ (g_i)_(i in I) (g'_i)_(i in I):=(g_i g'_i)_(i in I) $ then $display(product_(i in I)G_i)$ is a group with unit $= (1_(G_i))_(i in I), (g_i)_(i in I)^(-1)=(g_i^(-1))_(i in I).$

We call $display(product_(i in I)G_i)$ the direct product of $(G_i)_(i in I).$ \ If $forall i, G_i=G,$ then we write $G^I.$ If $I={1,dots,n}$ is finite, write $G_1 times dots times G_n.$

This construction is "associative" (by removing brackets): $ (G_1 times G_2) times G_3 attach(<->,t:"1:1") G_1 times G_2 times G_3 attach(<->,t:"1:1") G_1 times (G_2 times G_3). $

How do we show that they are the "same" groups?

== $section$ Homomorphisms and isomorphisms

General paradigm: homomorphism "are" structure-preserving maps.

*Definition* $quad G,G'$ groups. A map $f:G->G'$ is a homomorphism if $ f(x y)=f(x)f(y),forall x,y in G. $

*Properties* $quad$ If $f$ is a homomorphism, then 

- $f(1_G)=1_G', because f(1_G)=f(1_G 1_G)=f(1_G)f(1_G).$

- $f(x^(-1))=f(x)^(-1), because f(x^(-1))f(x)=f(1_G)=f(x)f(x^(-1)).$

*Remark* $quad$ Homomorphism between semigroups $f(x y)=f(x)f(y),$

between monoid $f(x y)=f(x)f(y), f(1)=1$ (not automatic).

*Examples* $quad id_G:G->G;$ inclusion $H subset G$ ($H$ subgroup); $"sgn":"S"_n->{plus.minus 1};$ \ $det: "GL"(n,F)->F^times$ ($F$ field), $M_(n times n)(F)->F$ (homo. of monoid); 

$(G_i)_(i in I)$ family of groups, $forall j in I, p_j:display(product_(i in I)G_i ->G_j)$ (projection).

$G attach(-->,t:g) G' attach(-->,t:f) G'', f,g$ homomorphism $==>$ so is $f g$ by checking definition.

*Definition* $quad$ Let $f:G->G', g:G'->G$ be homomorphisms. If $g f=id_G$, we say $g$ is a left inverse of $f$. Right inverse is similar. We say they are mutually inverse if $f g=id_G', g f=id_G.$ Note that $g$ is determined by $f:g=f^(-1)$ as maps. In this case, we say $f$ is an isomorphism, $g=$ its inverse which is also an isomorphism.

*Examples* $quad G attach(arrow.tilde, b:id_G) G,$ composition of $arrow.tilde$ is still $arrow.tilde.$ 

Given $G,G',$ if $exists f:G->G'$ isomorphism, we say $G$ and $G'$ are isomorphic, write $G tilde.eq G'$.

*Proposition* $quad$ Let $f:G->G'$ be a bijective homomorphism, then $f$ is $tilde.eq$ of groups.

*Proof* $quad$ To check: the inverse map $f^(-1):G'->G$ (between sets) is a homomorphism. #h(1fr) $square.stroked$

